            <ArticleTitle>Limitations of questioning asthma to assess asthma control in general practice.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1153-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.rmed.2008.03.008</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">The monitoring of children with asthma in primary care is based on the occurrence and frequency of asthma symptoms. We questioned whether the current approach is adequate to identify all children in whom a sufficient level of asthma control is not achieved.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The aim of this study is to illustrate that in some children asthma was incorrectly considered controlled, because the children failed to report current symptoms of asthma.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">One hundred and nineteen children were identified with recent wheezing plus moderate or severe airway hyperresponsiveness. We analyzed whether these children reported current symptoms of asthma (as normally questioned during a routine visit).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">In 20 children (18%) current asthma symptoms were absent despite moderately or severe airway hyperresponsiveness and wheezing in the last year. In addition, the usage of controller medication was very poor.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">We conclude that the general practitioner has insufficient tools to adequately assess asthma control in all children. The assessment of airway hyperresponsiveness as an additional guide to manage asthma in children in general practice is recommended. In this way, better asthma control can be achieved.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
